So that first header: 

Byte 1: \x10 or 0001 0000
The first nibble is the "MQTT Control Packet Type", in this case "CONNECT" which the clinet sends as it's first statement, a desire to connect. Connect has no flags associated with it, and infact by spec that will always be all zeros. So \x10 can be considered the connect byte.

Byte 2: \x1e or 00011110 or 30
This is the remaining length byte. Because the first bit is not set this is the only byte in the remaining length header. It specifies 30 more bytes to come. 

Byte 3 and 4: \x00\x04, the packet identifier. Required for some packets, pub, sub, unsub, pub rec, pub ack, pub rel, pub comp, sub ack, unsub, unsub ack. It appears this can be any arbitrary number.

